<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khorshidi dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1184 CE – 1597 CE (approx.) · capital Khorramabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The atabegs of Lesser Luristan who ruled from Khorramabad for four centuries until Shah Abbas I ended their line.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-al-din-khurshid">Shuja al-Din Khurshid</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lur</a> chief, took control of Lur-i Kuchik (Lesser Luristan), the mountains north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karkheh-river">Karkheh</a>, around 1184, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-iraq">Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq</a> declined. His family, the Khurshidids, ruled the region from the fortress town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramabad">Khorramabad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The atabegs governed a society of semi-nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lur</a> tribes who moved between summer and winter pastures, collecting tribute in livestock and controlling the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. They submitted in turn to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, which recognised them as governors (valis). The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falak-ol-aflak">Falak-ol-Aflak</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramabad">Khorramabad</a> was their seat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1597 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> had the last atabeg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahverdi-khan-khorshidi">Shahverdi Khan</a>, executed and replaced the dynasty with the Safavid-appointed valis of Luristan, a related family who ruled into the 20th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khorshidi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Khorshidi dynasty" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge (Great Britain) Engraver or Printer Murchison, I. Engraver or Printer L... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persia_with_part_of_the_Ottoman_Empire,_1872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1184 CE – 1597 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khorramabad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shuja al-Din Khurshid, Shahverdi Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khurshidids, Atabegs of Lur-i Kuchik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=khorshidi-dynasty" aria-label="Khorshidi dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5915 963 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khorshidi dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6235 1219L6134 1224M6235 1219L6281 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6281 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6235 1219h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6235 1219h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khorramabad. <a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=khorshidi-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

